@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.598 = private unnamed_addr constant [905 x i8] c" ## What it does\0A Checks for various invalid `@overload` usages.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A The `@overload` decorator is used to define functions and methods that accepts different\0A combinations of arguments and return different types based on the arguments passed. This is\0A mainly beneficial for type checkers. But, if the `@overload` usage is invalid, the type\0A checker may not be able to provide correct type information.\0A\0A ## Example\0A\0A Defining only one overload:\0A\0A ```py\0A from typing import overload\0A\0A @overload\0A def foo(x: int) -> int: ...\0A def foo(x: int | None) -> int | None:\0A     return x\0A ```\0A\0A Or, not providing an implementation for the overloaded definition:\0A\0A ```py\0A from typing import overload\0A\0A @overload\0A def foo() -> None: ...\0A @overload\0A def foo(x: int) -> int: ...\0A ```\0A\0A ## References\0A - [Python documentation: `@overload`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.overload)\0A", align 1
