@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.604 = private unnamed_addr constant [951 x i8] c" Checks for `raise` statements that raise non-exceptions or use invalid\0A causes for their raised exceptions.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A Only subclasses or instances of `BaseException` can be raised.\0A For an exception's cause, the same rules apply, except that `None` is also\0A permitted. Violating these rules results in a `TypeError` at runtime.\0A\0A ## Examples\0A ```python\0A def f():\0A     try:\0A         something()\0A     except NameError:\0A         raise \22oops!\22 from f\0A\0A def g():\0A     raise NotImplemented from 42\0A ```\0A\0A Use instead:\0A ```python\0A def f():\0A     try:\0A         something()\0A     except NameError as e:\0A         raise RuntimeError(\22oops!\22) from e\0A\0A def g():\0A     raise NotImplementedError from None\0A ```\0A\0A ## References\0A - [Python documentation: The `raise` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#raise)\0A - [Python documentation: Built-in Exceptions](https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html#built-in-exceptions)\0A", align 1
