@anon.b8a8f256d6b0dabde04650efd080b696.655 = private unnamed_addr constant [1197 x i8] c" ## What it does\0A Detects invalid `super()` calls where implicit arguments like the enclosing class or first method argument are unavailable.\0A\0A ## Why is this bad?\0A When `super()` is used without arguments, Python tries to find two things:\0A the nearest enclosing class and the first argument of the immediately enclosing function (typically self or cls).\0A If either of these is missing, the call will fail at runtime with a `RuntimeError`.\0A\0A ## Examples\0A ```python\0A super()  # error: no enclosing class or function found\0A\0A def func():\0A     super()  # error: no enclosing class or first argument exists\0A\0A class A:\0A     f = super()  # error: no enclosing function to provide the first argument\0A\0A     def method(self):\0A         def nested():\0A             super()  # error: first argument does not exist in this nested function\0A\0A         lambda: super()  # error: first argument does not exist in this lambda\0A\0A         (super() for _ in range(10))  # error: argument is not available in generator expression\0A\0A         super()  # okay! both enclosing class and first argument are available\0A ```\0A\0A ## References\0A - [Python documentation: super()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#super)\0A", align 1
